# This repository is byte-exact by design, so newline translation is a
# correctness bug here, not a cosmetic preference.
#
# examples/reference-package carries sha256 + byte-count pins that the
# conformance checker enforces (rule M013), and the worked-cell datasets are
# verified by re-running their committed generator and diffing the output.
# A newline filter on the way out of the object store breaks both.
#
# Measured, not assumed: cloning this repo on a machine with core.autocrlf=true
# and no .gitattributes rewrites examples/reference-package/predict.py from
# 2418 to 2484 bytes (66 CRLF conversions) and all four M013 pins fail. The
# first thing a new user does is clone and run the checker, so that is the
# worst possible place for the standard to appear broken.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files conventionally want CRLF, and a Windows-first repository would say
# `*.bat text eol=crlf` for that reason. That rule is wrong
# *here*: PROVENANCE.yaml records a content digest per file, and a file whose
# bytes change on checkout can never match its record -- a fresh clone failed
# test_recorded_digests_match_what_is_on_disk on exactly this file and nothing
# else. Byte-exactness is this repository's defining property, so it wins.
#
# Checked rather than assumed: the one .bat here is a linear launcher with no
# labels and no `goto`, which is where LF line endings actually bite cmd.exe.
*.bat -text

# Belt and braces for the hash-pinned artifacts: `-text` disables newline
# conversion outright, so these bytes are identical regardless of what any
# consumer's core.autocrlf is set to.
examples/reference-package/** -text
tests/fixtures/producer_packages/** -text
examples/worked-cells/**/data/*.csv -text

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
